var e={id:`cloud-computing`,number:1,domainId:`cloud-concepts`,title:`Cloud computing`,outlineHeading:`Describe cloud computing`,objectives:[{id:`1.1`,title:`Define cloud computing`,keyTerms:[`definition`],notes:{explanation:`Cloud computing is the delivery of computing services over the internet. Those services include common IT infrastructure such as virtual machines, storage, databases, and networking, and they extend to newer offerings like Internet of Things (IoT), machine learning (ML), and artificial intelligence (AI). Because the services come over the internet, you are not limited by the physical infrastructure of your own datacenter. If you need more capacity quickly, you do not wait months to buy and install servers. You provision it on demand and release it when you are done. For the exam, think of cloud computing as a shift from long procurement cycles to on-demand provisioning. That shift is what makes the other benefits possible: paying only for what you use, scaling with demand, and reaching users worldwide without building your own datacenters.`,keyFacts:[`Cloud computing = computing services (compute, storage, databases, networking, and more) delivered over the internet.`,`It also covers services like IoT, machine learning, and AI, not only traditional infrastructure.`,`Capacity is provisioned on demand instead of waiting to buy and install hardware.`],confusedWith:[{term:`On-premises datacenter`,difference:`On-premises, you buy, house, and maintain all the infrastructure yourself; in the cloud, a provider runs it and you rent services over the internet.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Cloud computing`,b:`On-premises datacenter`,hint:`Cloud: rent services online. On-prem: you own and run it all.`,aClue:`Rent services from a provider over the internet and pay as you use them`,bClue:`You buy, house, and maintain all the infrastructure yourself`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-compute/3-what-cloud-compute`}},{id:`1.2`,title:`Describe the shared responsibility model`,keyTerms:[`shared responsibility model`],notes:{explanation:`The shared responsibility model describes how duties are split between you and the cloud provider. In an on-premises datacenter you are responsible for everything, from the building and power to the servers, software, and data. In the cloud, the provider always owns the physical layer: the physical datacenter, the physical network, and the physical hosts. You always keep three things, whatever service you use: the information and data you store, the devices allowed to connect, and the accounts and identities in your environment. Everything in between, such as operating systems, network controls, applications, and identity and directory infrastructure, depends on the service type. IaaS leaves you the most responsibility, SaaS the least, and PaaS sits in the middle. Example: with a managed cloud SQL database the provider maintains the database, but you still own the data in it.`,keyFacts:[`The customer is ALWAYS responsible for: information and data, devices that connect, and accounts and identities.`,`The provider is ALWAYS responsible for: the physical datacenter, physical network, and physical hosts.`,`Operating systems, network controls, applications, and identity and directory infrastructure vary by service type.`,`Customer responsibility is highest with IaaS and lowest with SaaS; PaaS is in between.`],confusedWith:[{term:`SaaS means the provider handles everything`,difference:`Even with SaaS you never hand off your data, devices, or accounts and identities; those always stay with you.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Provider (in SaaS)`,b:`Customer (always)`,hint:`Even in SaaS, data, devices, and accounts stay with the customer.`,aClue:`Runs the application and the platform under it in a finished-app model`,bClue:`Keeps the data, devices, and accounts, whatever the service type`}},{term:`Accounts and identities vs identity and directory infrastructure`,difference:`Accounts and identities (your users, services, and devices) are always yours; the identity and directory infrastructure that runs them shifts toward Microsoft as you move from IaaS to SaaS.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Accounts & identities`,b:`Directory infra`,hint:`Accounts: always yours. Directory infra: shifts to Microsoft.`,aClue:`Your users, services, and devices, which always stay your responsibility`,bClue:`The system that runs sign-in, which shifts toward Microsoft as you move to SaaS`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-compute/4-describe-shared-responsibility-model`,verify:`Microsoft labels this row differently in different places. The shared responsibility diagram in the Azure docs says “identity and directory infrastructure”; the Learn training unit’s text says “identity and access”; the same docs page’s newer table lists “identities and users” as always the customer’s. Expect the exam to use any of these wordings.`}},{id:`1.3`,title:`Define cloud models, including public, private, and hybrid`,keyTerms:[`public`,`private`,`hybrid`],notes:{explanation:`Cloud models describe how cloud resources are deployed. A public cloud is built, controlled, and maintained by a third-party provider, and anyone who wants to buy services can use it. You need no capital expenditure to scale and pay only for what you use, but you do not have complete control over resources and security. A private cloud is used by a single organization. It can run in your own datacenter or in a dedicated offsite one, even one run by a third party. It gives complete control, but you buy and maintain the hardware. A hybrid cloud connects public and private clouds in one interconnected environment and gives the most flexibility, because you decide where each application runs. Microsoft Learn also covers multicloud, which means using two or more public cloud providers, and Azure Arc, which helps manage all of these environments.`,keyFacts:[`Public: owned and run by a third-party provider and available to anyone; no CapEx to scale, but less control.`,`Private: used by a single organization; complete control, but you buy and maintain the hardware.`,`Hybrid: public and private clouds interconnected; the most flexibility over where apps run.`,`Azure Arc helps manage public, private, hybrid, and multicloud environments.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Multicloud`,difference:`Hybrid combines public and private clouds; multicloud uses two or more public cloud providers.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Hybrid cloud`,b:`Multicloud`,hint:`Hybrid: public + private. Multicloud: 2+ public providers.`,aClue:`Combines public and private environments`,bClue:`Uses two or more public providers`}},{term:`Private cloud = on-premises only`,difference:`A private cloud can also be hosted in a dedicated offsite datacenter, even by a third party; single-organization use is what makes it private.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Private cloud`,b:`On-premises datacenter`,hint:`Private means one organization; it can be hosted offsite.`,exclude:`The two overlap (a private cloud can run on-premises), so no clue is true of only one side.`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-compute/5-define-cloud-models`}},{id:`1.4`,title:`Identify appropriate use cases for each cloud model`,keyTerms:[`use cases`],notes:{explanation:`Match the model to the requirement in the question. Choose public cloud when the priority is avoiding up-front hardware costs, provisioning and removing resources quickly, and paying only for what you use. Choose private cloud when an organization needs complete control over resources and security, or must keep its data apart from other tenants, and accepts buying and maintaining hardware. Choose hybrid cloud when an organization wants to keep some workloads private and run others in the public cloud. Examples include letting a private cloud surge into the public cloud for temporary demand, or keeping some services private for security, compliance, or legal requirements. Choose multicloud when an organization uses features from more than one public provider, or is migrating from one provider to another. If a company already runs VMware privately, Azure VMware Solution lets it run those workloads in Azure.`,keyFacts:[`Temporary demand spikes on top of a private cloud → hybrid (surge into public cloud).`,`Need to control where some workloads run for security, compliance, or legal reasons while still using public cloud → hybrid.`,`Complete control, no shared tenancy, hardware cost accepted → private.`,`No up-front hardware cost, pay only for what you use → public.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Hybrid for any mix of clouds`,difference:`Using two public providers (for example, Azure plus another public cloud) is multicloud, not hybrid.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Two public clouds`,b:`Hybrid cloud`,hint:`Azure plus another public cloud is multicloud, not hybrid.`,aClue:`Azure plus another provider's offering, with nothing private involved`,bClue:`Mixes a provider's shared services with private, single-organization ones`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-compute/5-define-cloud-models`}},{id:`1.5`,title:`Describe the consumption-based model`,keyTerms:[`consumption-based model`],notes:{explanation:`Cloud computing runs on a consumption-based model: you pay for the IT resources you use and nothing more. Instead of buying and maintaining a datacenter, you rent compute and storage and release them when you are done. This moves spending from capital expenditure (CapEx), which is up-front spending on physical infrastructure such as servers, network hardware, and datacenter space, to operational expenditure (OpEx), which is ongoing spending on services over time. Cloud computing is classified as OpEx. The model also changes capacity planning. In a traditional datacenter you must predict demand in advance: overestimate and you pay for idle hardware; underestimate and performance suffers until new hardware is ordered and installed. In the cloud you add resources when demand rises and release them when it falls.`,keyFacts:[`CapEx = up-front spending on physical infrastructure; OpEx = ongoing spending on services over time.`,`Cloud computing is classified as an operational expense (OpEx).`,`No up-front hardware costs and no paying for idle, underused capacity.`,`Add resources when demand rises; release them when it falls.`],confusedWith:[{term:`CapEx`,difference:`CapEx is a large up-front purchase, like buying servers; cloud spending is OpEx because you pay as you consume.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`CapEx`,b:`OpEx`,hint:`CapEx: up-front purchase. OpEx: pay as you consume (cloud).`,aClue:`A large up-front purchase, like buying servers`,bClue:`Pay as you consume, the way cloud spending works`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-compute/6-describe-consumption-based-model`}},{id:`1.6`,title:`Compare cloud pricing models`,keyTerms:[`cloud pricing models`],notes:{explanation:`The AZ-900 Learn path describes the cloud’s default pricing as pay-as-you-go: you pay only for the services you consume, which helps you plan operating costs, run efficiently, and scale as needs change. Azure documentation adds commitment-based options. Azure Reservations commit you to one-year or three-year plans for specific resources in exchange for a discount, and they suit stable, predictable usage. An Azure savings plan for compute commits you to a fixed hourly spend for one or three years; the lower prices apply automatically across eligible compute services and regions, so it suits changing workloads. Azure Spot Virtual Machines use Azure’s unused capacity at a significant discount, but Azure can evict them whenever it needs the capacity back. They only suit workloads that can handle interruptions, such as batch processing or dev/test.`,keyFacts:[`Pay-as-you-go: no commitment; you pay only for what you consume.`,`Reservations: 1- or 3-year commitment to a specific resource, size, and region; best for stable usage.`,`Savings plan for compute: 1- or 3-year hourly spend commitment that applies across eligible compute services and regions.`,`Spot VMs: unused capacity at a discount that can be evicted at any time, so only for interruptible workloads.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Reservations vs savings plan`,difference:`A reservation locks in a specific resource, size, and region; a savings plan commits to an hourly spend that applies flexibly across eligible compute.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Reservation`,b:`Savings plan`,hint:`Reservation: set resource, size, region. Plan: hourly spend.`,aClue:`Locks in a specific resource, size, and region`,bClue:`Commits to an hourly spend that applies flexibly across eligible compute`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/cost-management-billing/savings-plan/savings-plan-compute-overview`,verify:`The AZ-900 Learn path covers only pay-as-you-go pricing, so reservations, savings plans, and Spot VMs come from Azure docs rather than the training. Check how deeply the exam covers them.`}},{id:`1.7`,title:`Describe serverless`,keyTerms:[`serverless`],notes:{explanation:`Serverless computing lets you run code without provisioning or maintaining virtual machines or containers. The provider manages the infrastructure, and your code runs only when something triggers it. Azure Functions is Azure’s event-driven, serverless compute option. An event, such as a REST request, a timer, or a message from another Azure service, wakes the function, and when the function finishes, Azure deallocates the resources. Functions scale automatically with demand, so they fit well when demand is variable and the work completes quickly. Because nothing has to stay running between events, you are charged only for the CPU time used while the function runs. Functions are stateless by default; Durable Functions add state by passing context between runs. For the exam: serverless still runs on servers. You just do not manage them.`,keyFacts:[`Serverless: no VMs or containers to manage; code runs when an event triggers it.`,`Azure Functions is Azure’s event-driven, serverless compute option.`,`Scales automatically with demand; you are charged only for compute time while the code runs.`,`Functions are stateless by default; Durable Functions are stateful.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Virtual machines and containers`,difference:`VMs and containers must stay running for your app to work; a serverless function runs only when an event wakes it.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Serverless function`,b:`VM or container`,hint:`VMs and containers stay running; a function runs on an event.`,aClue:`Runs only when an event wakes it`,bClue:`Must stay running for your app to work`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-azure-compute-networking-services/6-functions`,verify:`Billing depends on the Azure Functions hosting plan. “Charged only for CPU time while the function runs” is how the Learn unit describes it; other hosting plans bill differently.`}}]},t={id:`cloud-benefits`,number:2,domainId:`cloud-concepts`,title:`Benefits of cloud services`,outlineHeading:`Describe the benefits of using cloud services`,objectives:[{id:`2.1`,title:`Describe the benefits of high availability and scalability in the cloud`,keyTerms:[`high availability`,`scalability`],notes:{explanation:`High availability is about keeping resources available when they are needed, regardless of disruptions or events. Azure services come with uptime guarantees defined in service-level agreements (SLAs), and the guarantee depends on the service. Scalability is the ability to adjust resources to meet demand. When traffic peaks you add resources; when demand drops you remove them, and because the cloud is consumption-based you stop paying for what you removed. Scaling comes in two types. Vertical scaling changes the capability of a resource: scale up by adding CPUs or RAM to a virtual machine, or scale down by reducing them. Horizontal scaling changes the number of resources: scale out by adding virtual machines or containers, or scale in by removing them, manually or automatically. Elasticity is scaling that happens automatically, in real time, based on actual demand.`,keyFacts:[`High availability = maximum uptime despite disruptions; Azure’s uptime guarantees are in SLAs.`,`Vertical scaling = change the size (scale up/down: more or less CPU or RAM).`,`Horizontal scaling = change the count (scale out/in: more or fewer VMs or containers).`,`Elasticity = resources adjust automatically, in real time, to actual demand.`],confusedWith:[{term:`High availability vs scalability`,difference:`High availability keeps a service up despite failures; scalability adjusts capacity to match demand.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`High availability`,b:`Scalability`,hint:`HA: stay up despite failures. Scalability: match demand.`,aClue:`Keeps a service up despite failures`,bClue:`Adjusts capacity to match demand`}},{term:`Scalability vs elasticity`,difference:`Scalability is the ability to add or remove resources, often planned or manual; elasticity is automatic, real-time adjustment to actual demand.`,source:`microsoft`,match:{a:`Scalability`,b:`Elasticity`,hint:`Elasticity is automatic, real-time scaling to actual demand.`,exclude:`Learn says scaling can be manual or automatic, so "automatic, real-time adjustment" doesn't cleanly separate the two.`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-benefits-use-cloud-services/2-high-availability-scalability-cloud`}},{id:`2.2`,title:`Describe the benefits of reliability and predictability in the cloud`,keyTerms:[`reliability`,`predictability`],notes:{explanation:`Reliability is the ability of a system to recover from failures and continue to function. It is one of the pillars of the Microsoft Azure Well-Architected Framework. The cloud’s decentralized design supports reliability: you can deploy resources in regions around the world, so if one region has a catastrophic event, other regions keep running. In some cases Azure shifts to another region for you automatically. Predictability lets you move forward with confidence, and it comes in two forms. Performance predictability means predicting the resources needed to give customers a good experience, supported by autoscaling, load balancing, and high availability. Cost predictability means forecasting cloud spend: tracking usage in real time, monitoring for efficiency, analyzing patterns and trends, and estimating costs with tools like the Azure Pricing Calculator.`,keyFacts:[`Reliability = recover from failures and keep functioning; a pillar of the Well-Architected Framework.`,`Deploying across global regions supports reliability: if one region fails, others keep running.`,`Predictability has two forms: performance predictability and cost predictability.`,`Performance: autoscaling, load balancing, high availability. Cost: real-time tracking, analytics, Pricing Calculator.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Reliability vs predictability`,difference:`Reliability is about recovering from failure; predictability is about knowing in advance what performance and cost you will get.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Reliability`,b:`Predictability`,hint:`Reliability: recover from failure. Predictability: forecast.`,aClue:`Recovering from failures and continuing to function`,bClue:`Knowing in advance what performance and cost you'll get`}},{term:`Reliability vs high availability`,difference:`High availability focuses on uptime; reliability focuses on recovering from failures and continuing to function.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Reliability`,b:`High availability`,hint:`HA focuses on uptime; reliability on recovering from failure.`,aClue:`Focuses on recovering from a failure and carrying on`,bClue:`Focuses on uptime`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-benefits-use-cloud-services/3-reliability-predictability-cloud`}},{id:`2.3`,title:`Describe the benefits of security and governance in the cloud`,keyTerms:[`security`,`governance`],notes:{explanation:`Cloud features support governance and compliance whether you use IaaS, PaaS, or SaaS. Templates help make sure deployed resources meet your technical standards and regulatory requirements, and when standards change you can update resources at scale. Cloud-based auditing flags resources that are out of compliance with your baseline and suggests how to fix them. Depending on your operating model, patches and updates may be applied automatically, which helps both governance and security. For security, you pick the model that matches the control you want. IaaS gives maximum control: the provider runs the physical resources, but you manage the operating system and installed software, including patches and maintenance. PaaS and SaaS take care of patches and maintenance for you. Cloud providers are also well suited to handle attacks such as distributed denial of service (DDoS).`,keyFacts:[`Governance: templates enforce standards, auditing flags non-compliant resources, and updates apply at scale.`,`Want maximum security control → IaaS (you patch the OS and installed software).`,`Want patches and maintenance handled for you → PaaS or SaaS.`,`Cloud providers are well suited to handle DDoS attacks.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Security vs governance`,difference:`Security protects resources from threats; governance keeps resources in line with your standards and compliance requirements.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Security`,b:`Governance`,hint:`Security: protect from threats. Governance: follow standards.`,aClue:`Protects resources from threats such as DDoS attacks`,bClue:`Keeps resources in line with your standards and compliance rules`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-benefits-use-cloud-services/4-security-governance-cloud`}},{id:`2.4`,title:`Describe the benefits of manageability in the cloud`,keyTerms:[`manageability`],notes:{explanation:`Microsoft Learn splits cloud manageability into two types. Management of the cloud is about managing the resources themselves. In the cloud you can automatically scale deployments based on need, deploy from preconfigured templates instead of configuring by hand, monitor resource health and automatically replace failing resources, and receive automatic alerts based on metrics you configure, so you see performance in real time. Management in the cloud is about how you manage your environment, meaning the tools you use: a web portal, a command-line interface, APIs, or PowerShell. In practice, an operations team might deploy resources from templates, watch their health in the portal, and automate recurring tasks with CLI or PowerShell scripts. Combining these reduces manual effort and helps keep configurations consistent.`,keyFacts:[`Management OF the cloud = managing resources: autoscaling, templates, health monitoring with auto-replacement, alerts.`,`Management IN the cloud = the tools: web portal, command-line interface, APIs, PowerShell.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Management of vs management in the cloud`,difference:`“Of” is what you can do to resources (scale, monitor, replace); “in” is how you do it (portal, CLI, APIs, PowerShell).`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Management of cloud`,b:`Management in cloud`,hint:`Of: scale, monitor, replace. In: portal, CLI, APIs, PowerShell.`,aClue:`What you can do to resources: scale, monitor, or replace them`,bClue:`How you do it: portal, command line, APIs, or PowerShell`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-benefits-use-cloud-services/5-manageability-cloud`}}]},n={id:`cloud-service-types`,number:3,domainId:`cloud-concepts`,title:`Cloud service types`,outlineHeading:`Describe cloud service types`,objectives:[{id:`3.1`,title:`Describe infrastructure as a service (IaaS)`,keyTerms:[`IaaS`],notes:{explanation:`Infrastructure as a service (IaaS) is the most flexible cloud service type and gives you the most control over your cloud resources. The provider maintains the hardware, network connectivity to the internet, and physical security. You are responsible for everything else, including installing, configuring, and maintaining the operating system; network configuration; and database and storage configuration. In effect, you are renting hardware in a cloud datacenter, and what you do with it is up to you. That flexibility comes with the largest operational responsibility of the three service types, because patching and many security controls are yours. On the exam, clues like “full control of the operating system”, “install custom software on a virtual machine”, or “move existing servers as they are” point to IaaS.`,keyFacts:[`Most flexible and most control, and also the most customer responsibility.`,`Provider: hardware, internet connectivity, physical security. You: OS, network configuration, database and storage configuration.`,`You are essentially renting hardware in a cloud datacenter.`],confusedWith:[{term:`PaaS`,difference:`With IaaS you manage the operating system; with PaaS the provider manages the OS, middleware, and runtime.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`IaaS`,b:`PaaS`,hint:`IaaS: you manage the OS. PaaS: provider manages OS and runtime.`,aClue:`You manage the operating system`,bClue:`The provider manages the OS, middleware, and runtime`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-service-types/2-describe-infrastructure-service`}},{id:`3.2`,title:`Describe platform as a service (PaaS)`,keyTerms:[`PaaS`],notes:{explanation:`Platform as a service (PaaS) is the middle ground between renting infrastructure (IaaS) and paying for a complete, deployed solution (SaaS). The provider maintains the physical infrastructure, physical security, and internet connection, plus the operating systems, middleware, development tools, and analytics services that make up the platform. You do not have to worry about licensing or patching operating systems and databases. You focus on your application code, your data, and access controls; depending on the service, some networking and application security settings are shared. PaaS is well suited to giving developers a complete development environment without the work of maintaining the infrastructure behind it. Built-in cloud features such as scalability, high availability, and multitenant capability reduce the amount of code developers have to write.`,keyFacts:[`Provider manages the OS, middleware, runtimes, and development tools, plus everything physical.`,`You manage your application code, data, and access controls.`,`No OS or database licensing or patching for you to handle.`],confusedWith:[{term:`SaaS`,difference:`PaaS gives you a platform to build and run your own application; SaaS gives you a finished application to use.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`PaaS`,b:`SaaS`,hint:`PaaS: a platform to build your app. SaaS: a finished app.`,aClue:`A platform to build and run your own application`,bClue:`A finished application you just use`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-service-types/3-describe-platform-service`}},{id:`3.3`,title:`Describe software as a service (SaaS)`,keyTerms:[`SaaS`],notes:{explanation:`Software as a service (SaaS) is the most complete cloud service type from a product perspective: you rent or use a fully developed application. Email, financial software, messaging applications, and connectivity software are common examples. SaaS is the least flexible model, but it is the easiest to get running and needs the least technical knowledge. The provider manages almost the whole application stack, including infrastructure, platform, and application maintenance such as patching, updates, and availability. You mainly manage your data, identity and access settings, and which devices can access the service. That gives SaaS the lowest operational overhead of the three types. For example, a team using a SaaS collaboration platform focuses on onboarding users, access controls, and data governance, while the provider handles everything underneath.`,keyFacts:[`A fully developed application you use, such as email, messaging, productivity, or finance software.`,`Least flexible, but the easiest to start and the least technical expertise needed.`,`You still manage your data, identity and access settings, and device access.`],confusedWith:[{term:`The provider handles everything`,difference:`Even in SaaS, your data, accounts and identities, and devices stay your responsibility.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`SaaS provider`,b:`SaaS customer`,hint:`The customer still owns data, accounts, and devices.`,exclude:`Same distinction as 1.2's provider/customer pair, so it would repeat those cards.`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-service-types/4-describe-software-service`}},{id:`3.4`,title:`Identify appropriate use cases for each cloud service type (IaaS, PaaS, and SaaS)`,keyTerms:[`use cases for each`],notes:{explanation:`The exam often describes a scenario and asks which service type fits. Pick IaaS for a lift-and-shift migration, where you set up cloud resources like your on-premises datacenter and move workloads over, and for testing and development environments you need to start and stop quickly while keeping complete control. Pick PaaS when developers need a development framework to build or customize cloud applications without managing the underlying infrastructure, or for analytics and business intelligence tools delivered as a service. Pick SaaS when people just need a ready-made application: email and messaging, productivity applications, or finance and expense tracking. A quick rule: the more control over the operating system a scenario needs, the closer it is to IaaS. The less the organization wants to manage, the closer it is to SaaS.`,keyFacts:[`IaaS: lift-and-shift migration; testing and development environments.`,`PaaS: development frameworks; analytics or business intelligence.`,`SaaS: email and messaging; productivity applications; finance and expense tracking.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Lift-and-shift as PaaS`,difference:`Lift-and-shift keeps your existing servers and operating systems as they are, so it is IaaS; with PaaS the provider takes over the OS and runtime.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Lift-and-shift`,b:`PaaS`,hint:`Lift-and-shift keeps your servers and OS as-is, so it's IaaS.`,aClue:`Keeps existing servers and operating systems as they are`,bClue:`The provider takes over the OS and runtime`}}],learnUrl:`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cloud-service-types/`}}]},r=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-core-architectural-components-of-azure/5-describe-azure-physical-infrastructure`,i=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-core-architectural-components-of-azure/6-describe-azure-management-infrastructure`,a={id:`core-architecture`,number:4,domainId:`architecture-services`,title:`Core architecture`,outlineHeading:`Describe the core architectural components of Azure`,objectives:[{id:`4.1`,title:`Describe Azure regions, region pairs, and sovereign regions`,keyTerms:[`regions`,`region pairs`,`sovereign regions`],notes:{explanation:`A region is a geographical area that contains one or more datacenters that are close together and connected by a low-latency network. You choose a region for most resources, and some services or VM sizes are only available in certain regions. A few services are global and need no region, such as Microsoft Entra ID, Azure Traffic Manager, and Azure DNS. Most regions are paired with another region in the same geography at least 300 miles away. Region pairs protect against events that take out a whole region, such as a natural disaster: updates roll out to one region of a pair at a time, and one region per pair is prioritized for recovery. Not every service replicates automatically, and some regions have no pair. Sovereign regions are isolated instances of Azure for compliance or legal needs, such as the US Government regions and the China regions operated by 21Vianet.`,keyFacts:[`Region = one or more nearby datacenters connected by a low-latency network.`,`Region pairs: usually in the same geography, at least 300 miles apart; they protect against a region-wide disaster. Not every region has one.`,`Paired regions get planned updates one region at a time, and one region per pair is prioritized for recovery.`,`Sovereign regions are isolated from the main Azure instance (US Government regions; China regions operated by 21Vianet).`],confusedWith:[{term:`Availability zones`,difference:`Availability zones protect against a datacenter failure inside one region; region pairs protect against losing an entire region.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Availability zone`,b:`Region pair`,hint:`Zone: survives a datacenter failure. Pair: a region loss.`,aClue:`Survives a datacenter failure without leaving the area`,bClue:`Two locations at least 300 miles apart in the same geography`}},{term:`Every region has a pair`,difference:`Not true: some regions (e.g., Italy North, Poland Central, Israel Central) have no traditional pair, and Brazil South is paired one way only, with South Central US outside its geography.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Paired region`,b:`Unpaired region`,hint:`Not every region has a pair; some rely on zones instead.`,aClue:`Planned updates roll out to it and its partner one at a time`,bClue:`Has no partner at all, inside its geography or outside it`}}],learnUrl:r}},{id:`4.2`,title:`Describe availability zones`,keyTerms:[`availability zones`],notes:{explanation:`Availability zones are physically separate datacenters within one Azure region. Each zone has one or more datacenters with independent power, cooling, and networking, and zones are connected by high-speed, private fiber-optic networks. A zone is an isolation boundary: if one zone goes down, the others keep working. Every region that supports availability zones has at least three of them, but not every region supports zones. You protect a workload by running copies of it in more than one zone, which can add cost for the duplicate resources and for data transfer between zones. Services that use zones fall into three groups. Zonal services are pinned to a specific zone, such as VMs, managed disks, and IP addresses. Zone-redundant services are replicated across zones automatically, such as zone-redundant storage and SQL Database. Non-regional services are always available.`,keyFacts:[`Availability zone = one or more physically separate datacenters in a region, with independent power, cooling, and networking.`,`Every zone-enabled region has a minimum of three availability zones; not all regions support zones.`,`Zonal: you pin a resource to one zone. Zone-redundant: Azure replicates it across zones for you.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Availability sets`,difference:`Availability sets spread VMs across fault and update domains to survive a hardware failure or maintenance event; zones survive a whole datacenter failing.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Availability zone`,b:`Availability set`,hint:`Set: hardware or maintenance failure. Zone: datacenter failure.`,aClue:`Survives a whole datacenter failing`,bClue:`Spreads VMs across fault and update domains`}}],learnUrl:r}},{id:`4.3`,title:`Describe Azure datacenters`,keyTerms:[`datacenters`],notes:{explanation:`Azure’s physical infrastructure starts with datacenters: facilities with servers arranged in racks, each with dedicated power, cooling, and networking. They are like an on-premises datacenter, but at a much larger scale, and Azure has them all around the world. You don’t interact with individual datacenters directly. Instead, Azure groups them into regions and availability zones, and that grouping is what gives your workloads resiliency and reliability. On the exam, treat the datacenter as the bottom layer of the physical infrastructure: datacenters make up availability zones and regions, and you pick a region (and sometimes a zone), never a specific building. Under the shared responsibility model, the physical datacenter is always Microsoft’s responsibility once you are in the cloud, and physical security of those buildings is the first layer of the defense-in-depth model.`,keyFacts:[`Datacenter = a facility with racks of servers plus dedicated power, cooling, and networking.`,`You never choose an individual datacenter; you choose a region, and optionally an availability zone.`,`Datacenters are grouped into availability zones and regions.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Availability zone`,difference:`A datacenter is a physical facility; an availability zone is one or more datacenters with independent power, cooling, and networking.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Datacenter`,b:`Availability zone`,hint:`A zone is one or more datacenters with independent power.`,aClue:`A single physical facility with racks of servers`,bClue:`One or more facilities with independent power, cooling, and networking`}}],learnUrl:r}},{id:`4.4`,title:`Describe Azure resources and resource groups`,keyTerms:[`resources`,`resource groups`],notes:{explanation:`A resource is the basic building block of Azure: anything you create, provision, or deploy, such as a VM, a virtual network, a database, or an Azure AI service. A resource group is a container for resources. Every resource must belong to exactly one resource group at a time. You can move some resources between groups, but resource groups can’t be nested and can’t be renamed after you create them. Actions you apply to a resource group apply to everything in it. Deleting a resource group deletes every resource inside it, and granting or denying access on the group applies to all its resources. That makes resource groups useful for lifecycle grouping: put a temporary dev environment in one group and delete the whole group when you’re done, or give each project its own group so each team manages only its own resources.`,keyFacts:[`Every resource belongs to exactly one resource group at a time.`,`Resource groups can’t be nested and can’t be renamed.`,`Deleting a resource group deletes all the resources in it.`,`Access granted or denied on a resource group applies to all its resources.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Subscription`,difference:`A resource group organizes resources; a subscription is the billing and access boundary that contains resource groups.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Resource group`,b:`Subscription`,hint:`A group organizes resources; a subscription is billed.`,aClue:`Every VM, database, or network sits directly in exactly one of these`,bClue:`The billing and access boundary; each gets its own invoice`}}],learnUrl:i}},{id:`4.5`,title:`Describe subscriptions`,keyTerms:[`subscriptions`],notes:{explanation:`A subscription is a unit of management, billing, and scale in Azure. You need one to use Azure, and it links to an Azure account, which is an identity in Microsoft Entra ID or in a directory that Entra ID trusts. An account can have several subscriptions. Each subscription is two kinds of boundary. It’s a billing boundary, because Azure produces separate billing reports and invoices for each subscription. It’s also an access control boundary, because access-management policies apply at the subscription level. Organizations add subscriptions to separate environments, such as development, test, and production; to separate teams or workloads so costs are easy to track; or to split billing, for example between production and dev/test. Subscriptions contain resource groups, and they can be organized into management groups.`,keyFacts:[`Subscription = a unit of management, billing, and scale.`,`Two boundaries: billing (separate invoices per subscription) and access control (policies applied at the subscription level).`,`One account can have multiple subscriptions, commonly split by environment, team, or billing needs.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure account`,difference:`The account is the identity in Microsoft Entra ID that signs in; the subscriptions linked to it provide access to services and are what get billed.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Azure account`,b:`Subscription`,hint:`Account: the identity that signs in. Subscription: billed.`,aClue:`The identity in Microsoft Entra ID that signs in`,bClue:`What gets billed for the services you use`}}],learnUrl:i}},{id:`4.6`,title:`Describe management groups`,keyTerms:[`management groups`],notes:{explanation:`Management groups sit above subscriptions. When an organization has many subscriptions across teams or geographies, it can organize them into management groups and apply governance conditions, such as access policies or compliance rules, to a whole group. Every subscription in the management group automatically inherits those conditions. Management groups can be nested up to six levels deep, not counting the root level or the subscription level, so the hierarchy can mirror the organization. Every Microsoft Entra tenant has a single top-level Tenant Root Group, and all other management groups and subscriptions fold up to it, which lets you apply policies globally. Each management group and subscription can have only one parent. Typical uses are applying an Azure Policy, such as allowed VM locations, across many subscriptions at once, or granting access to many subscriptions with one role assignment.`,keyFacts:[`Management groups organize subscriptions and apply policies and access to all of them at once.`,`They can be nested up to six levels deep (not counting the root level or the subscription level).`,`One Tenant Root Group per Microsoft Entra tenant; each management group or subscription has only one parent.`,`A single directory supports up to 10,000 management groups.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Resource groups`,difference:`Resource groups hold resources and can’t be nested; management groups hold subscriptions and can be nested.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Resource group`,b:`Management group`,hint:`Management groups hold subscriptions and can nest.`,aClue:`Holds VMs and databases directly and can't be nested`,bClue:`Holds subscriptions and can be nested up to six levels`}}],learnUrl:i}},{id:`4.7`,title:`Describe the hierarchy of resource groups, subscriptions, and management groups`,keyTerms:[`hierarchy`],notes:{explanation:`Azure’s management hierarchy has four levels, from top to bottom: management groups, subscriptions, resource groups, and resources. Settings flow downward. A policy or RBAC role assignment applied to a management group is inherited by every management group, subscription, resource group, and resource beneath it, and a resource or subscription owner can’t override a policy inherited from above. Resources likewise inherit settings from their resource group. Deletion flows downward too: deleting a resource group deletes all the resources in it. The structure is flexible. For example, you might put all production subscriptions under a Production management group with a policy that limits VM locations to one region, then grant access once at that group instead of in every subscription. Because each management group and subscription has only one parent, every resource has exactly one path up to the Tenant Root Group.`,keyFacts:[`Top to bottom: management groups → subscriptions → resource groups → resources.`,`Policies and RBAC role assignments are inherited by everything below the scope where they’re applied.`,`A resource or subscription owner can’t override a policy inherited from a management group.`,`Deleting a resource group deletes every resource in it.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Physical hierarchy`,difference:`Management groups → resources is how you organize and govern; datacenters, availability zones, and regions are where resources physically run.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Management hierarchy`,b:`Physical hierarchy`,hint:`Management: organize and govern. Physical: where things run.`,aClue:`How you organize and govern: groups, subscriptions, resources`,bClue:`Where things run: datacenters, zones, and regions`}}],learnUrl:i}}]},o=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-azure-compute-networking-services`,s=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-azure-networking-services`,c={id:`compute-networking`,number:5,domainId:`architecture-services`,title:`Compute and networking`,outlineHeading:`Describe Azure compute and networking services`,objectives:[{id:`5.1`,title:`Compare compute types, including containers, virtual machines, and functions`,keyTerms:[`containers vs VMs vs functions`],notes:{explanation:`Azure offers three main compute types. Virtual machines are infrastructure as a service: you control the operating system and installed software, which suits total OS control, custom software, or lift-and-shift migrations. Each VM runs its own operating system. Containers are a lighter-weight virtualization environment: many containers run on one host, you don’t manage their operating system, and they are created, scaled out, and stopped quickly. They suit microservice architectures, where you scale or update each part of an app independently. Azure Container Instances, Azure Container Apps, and Azure Kubernetes Service (AKS) run containers. Functions are serverless: Azure Functions runs code only when an event triggers it and charges for the compute time used while it runs, which suits short, event-driven tasks. Moving from VMs to containers to functions, you manage less and control less.`,keyFacts:[`VMs (IaaS): you manage the operating system; the most control; each VM runs its own OS.`,`Containers: many per host, no OS to manage, lightweight and quick to scale; a good fit for microservices.`,`Functions: serverless and event-driven; code runs only when triggered.`,`Container services: Container Instances (simplest, PaaS), Container Apps (PaaS with built-in load balancing and scaling), AKS (orchestration).`],confusedWith:[{term:`Containers vs VMs`,difference:`Each VM runs its own operating system that you manage; containers run on a shared host and you don’t manage their operating system.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Virtual machine`,b:`Container`,hint:`A VM runs its own OS you manage; containers share a host.`,aClue:`Runs its own operating system that you manage`,bClue:`Runs on a shared host; you don't manage its OS`}}],learnUrl:`${o}/5-containers`}},{id:`5.2`,title:`Describe virtual machine options, including Azure virtual machines, Azure Virtual Machine Scale Sets, availability sets, and Azure Virtual Desktop`,keyTerms:[`VMs`,`VM Scale Sets`,`availability sets`,`Azure Virtual Desktop`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Virtual Machines give you a virtualized server as IaaS, which is fine on its own for testing, development, or minor tasks. Virtual Machine Scale Sets create and manage a group of identical, load-balanced VMs from one central configuration, and they can scale out or in automatically based on demand or a schedule. Availability sets improve VM resiliency inside a region by spreading VMs across update domains, which are rebooted together during planned maintenance, and fault domains, which share a power or network failure point. They cost nothing extra; you pay only for the VMs. In regions with availability zones, zone-based designs are often preferred because they isolate failures more broadly. Azure Virtual Desktop is different: it’s a managed desktop and app virtualization service that lets users securely access Windows desktops and apps from many devices, with single-session or multi-session options.`,keyFacts:[`Scale sets: identical, load-balanced VMs that scale automatically to handle changing demand.`,`Availability sets: spread VMs across update and fault domains to survive maintenance events and hardware failures within a region.`,`Availability sets add no cost; you pay only for the VM instances.`,`Azure Virtual Desktop: cloud-hosted Windows desktops and apps, single- or multi-session, with Microsoft Entra ID for access.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Scale sets vs availability sets`,difference:`Scale sets change how many VMs run to meet demand; availability sets spread VMs so one failure or maintenance event doesn’t take them all down.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Scale set`,b:`Availability set`,hint:`A scale set changes VM count; an availability set spreads VMs.`,aClue:`Changes how many VMs run to meet demand`,bClue:`Spreads VMs so one hardware failure doesn't take them all down`}},{term:`What each option protects against`,difference:`Availability set: hardware or maintenance failure. Availability zone: datacenter failure. Region pair: region-wide disaster. Scale set: mainly scaling with demand, though several load-balanced instances also improve availability.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Availability set`,b:`Region pair`,hint:`Set: hardware or maintenance failure. Pair: region disaster.`,aClue:`Survives a hardware failure or maintenance event`,bClue:`Survives losing an entire Azure location, such as in a natural disaster`}}],learnUrl:`${o}/2-virtual-machines`}},{id:`5.3`,title:`Describe the resources required for virtual machines`,keyTerms:[`resources a VM needs`],notes:{explanation:`When you create a VM, you choose the resources it needs. The main ones are a size, which sets the VM’s purpose, number of processor cores, and amount of RAM; storage disks, such as hard disk drives or solid-state drives; and networking, meaning a virtual network, a public IP address, and port configuration. VMs are usually deployed from an image, which is a template that already includes an operating system and tools. VM sizes are grouped into families by workload, for example general purpose (D-series), memory optimized (E-series), compute optimized (F-series), and GPU enabled (N-series). The size name carries information too: in Standard_D2s_v5, D is the family, 2 is the number of vCPUs, s means Premium SSD support, and v5 is the hardware generation. At a fundamentals level, pick a family that matches the workload, then a size that meets its performance needs.`,keyFacts:[`A VM needs a size (processor cores and RAM), storage disks, and networking (virtual network, public IP address, ports).`,`VMs are deployed from images: templates that include an operating system and tools.`,`Sizes are grouped into families by workload, such as general purpose, memory optimized, compute optimized, and GPU enabled.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure Disks vs Blob storage`,difference:`A VM’s disks are Azure Disks, block-level volumes for the VM; Blob storage is object storage reached over HTTP or HTTPS.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Azure Disks`,b:`Blob storage`,hint:`Disks: block volumes for VMs. Blobs: objects over HTTP(S).`,aClue:`Block-level volumes attached to a VM`,bClue:`Object data reached over HTTP or HTTPS`}}],learnUrl:`${o}/2-virtual-machines`}},{id:`5.4`,title:`Describe application hosting options, including web apps, containers, and virtual machines`,keyTerms:[`app hosting (web apps, containers, VMs)`],notes:{explanation:`Azure gives you three broad ways to host an application. Virtual machines give you maximum control of the hosting environment and may feel most familiar if you are new to the cloud. Containers let you isolate and manage different parts of the solution individually. Azure App Service is the platform option: it hosts web apps, API apps, WebJobs for background tasks, and mobile app back ends in your choice of language, including .NET, Java, PHP, Python, and Node.js, on Windows or Linux, without you managing infrastructure. App Service is an HTTP-based service with built-in automatic scaling, load balancing, and high availability, and it supports continuous deployment from GitHub, Azure DevOps, or any Git repository. On the exam, choose VMs for full control, containers for independently managed components, and App Service when you just want to deploy web code and let Azure run the environment.`,keyFacts:[`App Service hosts web apps, API apps, WebJobs, and mobile back ends without you managing infrastructure.`,`App Service is HTTP-based, runs on Windows or Linux, and includes automatic scaling and high availability.`,`Choose VMs for maximum control, containers for isolated components, and App Service for web apps without infrastructure work.`],confusedWith:[{term:`App Service vs Azure Functions`,difference:`App Service hosts a web app or API that stays available to serve requests; Functions runs code only when an event triggers it.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`App Service`,b:`Azure Functions`,hint:`App Service stays available; Functions run on events.`,aClue:`Hosts a website or API that stays available for requests`,bClue:`Runs code only when an event triggers it`}}],learnUrl:`${o}/7-describe-application-hosting-options`}},{id:`5.5`,title:`Describe virtual networking, including the purpose of Azure virtual networks, subnets, peering, Azure DNS, Azure VPN Gateway, and ExpressRoute`,keyTerms:[`VNets`,`subnets`,`peering`,`Azure DNS`,`VPN Gateway`,`ExpressRoute`],notes:{explanation:`Azure virtual networks (VNets) let Azure resources communicate with each other, with the internet, and with on-premises networks. You define a private IP address space and divide it into subnets for isolation and segmentation. Virtual network peering connects two VNets directly, even across regions; the traffic is private and stays on the Microsoft backbone network. To reach on-premises, Azure VPN Gateway sends encrypted traffic over the public internet, either site-to-site for a whole network or point-to-site for one computer; each VNet can have only one VPN gateway. ExpressRoute instead gives a private connection through a connectivity provider that doesn’t travel over the public internet, with more reliability, faster speeds, and consistent latency. Azure DNS hosts DNS domains and provides name resolution on Azure infrastructure, but you can’t use it to buy a domain name.`,keyFacts:[`VNets are divided into subnets; peering connects VNets privately over the Microsoft backbone, even across regions.`,`VPN Gateway: encrypted tunnel over the public internet (site-to-site, point-to-site, VNet-to-VNet); one per VNet.`,`ExpressRoute: a private connection through a connectivity provider that doesn’t use the public internet.`,`Azure DNS hosts domains and records; you can’t buy a domain with it (use App Service domains or a third-party registrar).`],confusedWith:[{term:`VPN Gateway vs ExpressRoute`,difference:`VPN Gateway encrypts traffic across the public internet; ExpressRoute is a private connection that bypasses it.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`VPN Gateway`,b:`ExpressRoute`,hint:`VPN: encrypted over the internet. ExpressRoute: private.`,aClue:`Encrypts traffic across the public internet`,bClue:`A private connection through a provider that bypasses the internet`}},{term:`VNet peering vs VPN`,difference:`Peering links Azure VNets directly over the Microsoft backbone; a VPN encrypts traffic across the internet, typically to on-premises.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`VNet peering`,b:`VPN`,hint:`Peering: VNet to VNet on Microsoft's backbone, not the internet.`,aClue:`Links two virtual networks directly over the Microsoft backbone`,bClue:`Encrypts traffic over the internet, usually to on-premises`}}],learnUrl:`${s}/2-virtual-network`}},{id:`5.6`,title:`Define public and private endpoints`,keyTerms:[`public vs private endpoints`],notes:{explanation:`Azure virtual networking supports two kinds of endpoints for reaching resources. A public endpoint has a public IP address and can be accessed from anywhere in the world. A private endpoint exists inside a virtual network and has a private IP address from that virtual network’s address space. Azure’s documentation describes a private endpoint as bringing the service into your virtual network, so you reach it privately rather than through a public address. You can also make a resource reachable from the internet by assigning it a public IP address or placing it behind a public load balancer. Two related tools appear in the same Learn unit: service endpoints, which link Azure resources such as Azure SQL databases and storage accounts to virtual networks, and network security groups, which allow or block traffic by source and destination IP address, port, and protocol.`,keyFacts:[`Public endpoint: a public IP address, reachable from anywhere in the world.`,`Private endpoint: a private IP address from within your virtual network’s address space.`,`Network security groups allow or block traffic based on IP address, port, and protocol.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Service endpoints`,difference:`Service endpoints link services like Azure SQL and Storage to a VNet; a private endpoint brings the service into your VNet with its own private IP address.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Private endpoint`,b:`Service endpoint`,hint:`A private endpoint gives the service a private IP in your VNet.`,aClue:`Gets its own IP address from inside your virtual network`,bClue:`Links Azure SQL or Storage to a VNet without giving it an address there`}}],learnUrl:`${s}/2-virtual-network`}}]},l=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-azure-storage-services`,u={id:`storage`,number:6,domainId:`architecture-services`,title:`Storage`,outlineHeading:`Describe Azure storage services`,objectives:[{id:`6.1`,title:`Compare Azure Storage services`,keyTerms:[`storage services`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Storage has five core data services, and the exam often asks which one fits a kind of data. Azure Blobs is a massively scalable object store for unstructured text and binary data, such as images or documents served to a browser, streaming video and audio, backups, archives, and data for analysis. Azure Files offers fully managed file shares that cloud or on-premises machines can mount at the same time using SMB or NFS. Azure Queues stores large numbers of messages for asynchronous processing between application components, with each message up to 64 KB. Azure Disks provides block-level storage volumes for Azure VMs. Azure Tables is a NoSQL store for large amounts of structured, non-relational data. All of them are durable and highly available, encrypted by the service, massively scalable, managed by Azure, and accessible over HTTP or HTTPS.`,keyFacts:[`Blobs: unstructured objects such as media, documents, backups, logs, and analytics data.`,`Files: managed file shares mounted over SMB or NFS, from the cloud or on-premises.`,`Queues: messages between app components (up to 64 KB each). Tables: NoSQL, structured, non-relational data.`,`Disks: block-level volumes for Azure VMs.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Blobs vs Files`,difference:`Blobs are objects reached by URL or API; Files are shares you mount like a network drive over SMB or NFS.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Blob storage`,b:`Azure Files`,hint:`Blobs: unstructured objects. Files: shares over SMB or NFS.`,aClue:`Unstructured objects such as images, video, and backups`,bClue:`Shares you mount like a network drive over SMB or NFS`}},{term:`Tables vs a relational database`,difference:`Azure Tables is a NoSQL store for structured, non-relational data, not a relational SQL database.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Azure Tables`,b:`Relational database`,hint:`Azure Tables is NoSQL for non-relational data.`,aClue:`A NoSQL store for structured data`,bClue:`A SQL store built around relationships between records`}}],learnUrl:`${l}/4-describe-azure-storage-services`}},{id:`6.2`,title:`Describe storage tiers`,keyTerms:[`tiers`],notes:{explanation:`Blob storage offers access tiers that trade storage cost against access cost and speed. The hot tier is for data accessed frequently, such as website images. The cool tier is for infrequently accessed data stored for at least 30 days, such as customer invoices. The cold tier is for infrequently accessed data stored for at least 90 days. The archive tier is for rarely accessed data stored for at least 180 days with flexible latency needs, such as long-term backups. Cooler tiers cost less to store but more to access. Archive is the cheapest to store but is offline: an archived blob can’t be read or modified until you rehydrate it to the hot, cool, or cold tier, which at standard priority can take up to 15 hours. Hot, cool, and cold can be set as the account default; archive can only be set on individual blobs.`,keyFacts:[`Hot: frequent access. Cool: infrequent, at least 30 days. Cold: infrequent, at least 90 days. Archive: rare, at least 180 days.`,`Cooler tiers have lower storage costs but higher access costs.`,`Archive is offline: blobs must be rehydrated to hot, cool, or cold before reading (up to 15 hours at standard priority).`,`Archive can be set only on individual blobs, not as the account-level default.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Cool and cold vs archive`,difference:`Cool and cold are online, so you can read data any time at a higher access cost; archive is offline until you rehydrate it.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Cool or cold tier`,b:`Archive tier`,hint:`Cool and cold are online; archive is offline until rehydrated.`,aClue:`Online, so data can be read right away with no rehydration wait`,bClue:`Offline until the blob is rehydrated`}}],learnUrl:`${l}/4-describe-azure-storage-services`}},{id:`6.3`,title:`Describe redundancy options`,keyTerms:[`redundancy options`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Storage always keeps three copies of your data in the primary region. Locally redundant storage (LRS) keeps all three in a single datacenter; it costs the least and protects against drive and rack failures, but not a datacenter disaster. Zone-redundant storage (ZRS) copies data synchronously across three availability zones in the primary region, so data stays readable and writable if one zone fails. Geo-redundant storage (GRS) adds copies in the paired secondary region: LRS in the primary, then asynchronous replication to LRS in the secondary. Geo-zone-redundant storage (GZRS) uses ZRS in the primary and LRS in the secondary. By default, the secondary copy is usable only after a failover. The read-access variants, RA-GRS and RA-GZRS, let you read from the secondary region at any time, though that data may lag behind the primary.`,keyFacts:[`LRS: three copies in one datacenter. ZRS: three copies across three availability zones in the primary region.`,`GRS = LRS in the primary region plus copies in the secondary region. GZRS = ZRS in the primary plus copies in the secondary.`,`Secondary-region copies are asynchronous and not readable until failover, unless you choose RA-GRS or RA-GZRS.`],confusedWith:[{term:`GRS vs RA-GRS`,difference:`Both copy data to a secondary region; only RA-GRS lets you read the secondary copy before a failover.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`GRS`,b:`RA-GRS`,hint:`Only RA-GRS lets you read the secondary copy before failover.`,aClue:`Its second-region copy is usable only after a failover`,bClue:`Lets you read the second-region copy at any time`}},{term:`ZRS vs GRS`,difference:`ZRS protects against a zone failure within one region; GRS protects against losing the whole primary region.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`ZRS`,b:`GRS`,hint:`ZRS: survives a zone failure. GRS: survives a region loss.`,aClue:`Survives a zone failure but keeps every copy in one region`,bClue:`Copies data to a paired secondary region`}}],learnUrl:`${l}/3-redundancy`}},{id:`6.4`,title:`Describe storage account options and storage types`,keyTerms:[`storage account types`],notes:{explanation:`A storage account gives your Azure Storage data a unique namespace that’s reachable from anywhere over HTTP or HTTPS. The account type you pick decides which services and redundancy options you get. Standard general-purpose v2 supports Blob Storage (including Data Lake Storage), Queue Storage, Table Storage, and Azure Files with every redundancy option, and Microsoft recommends it for most scenarios. The premium types trade flexibility for performance: premium block blobs for high transaction rates or low latency, with LRS or ZRS; premium file shares for high-performance file shares, including NFS, with LRS or ZRS; and premium page blobs, which support only page blobs and LRS. Every storage account name must be unique across all of Azure, 3 to 24 characters long, and use only numbers and lowercase letters, because the name forms each service’s endpoint address.`,keyFacts:[`Standard general-purpose v2: blobs, files, queues, and tables with all redundancy options; recommended for most scenarios.`,`Premium types (block blobs, file shares, page blobs) are for high performance and support fewer redundancy options.`,`Account names are globally unique, 3–24 characters, lowercase letters and numbers only.`,`The account name forms the endpoint, for example https://<name>.blob.core.windows.net.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Storage account vs storage service`,difference:`The storage account is the container and namespace; Blobs, Files, Queues, and Tables are the services inside it.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Storage account`,b:`Storage service`,hint:`Account: the namespace. Blobs, Files, Queues, Tables: services.`,aClue:`The container and unique namespace for your data`,bClue:`Blobs, Files, Queues, or Tables`}}],learnUrl:`${l}/2-accounts`}},{id:`6.5`,title:`Identify options for moving files, including AzCopy, Azure Storage Explorer, and Azure File Sync`,keyTerms:[`AzCopy`,`Storage Explorer`,`Azure File Sync`],notes:{explanation:`Azure provides three tools for moving or working with individual files and small groups of files. AzCopy is a command-line utility that uploads, downloads, and copies blobs or files between storage accounts, and it can even work with other cloud providers. When AzCopy synchronizes, it works in one direction only: you name a source and a destination, and it copies in that direction. Azure Storage Explorer is a standalone app for Windows, macOS, and Linux that gives you a graphical interface for managing files and blobs; it uses AzCopy behind the scenes. Azure File Sync is installed on a Windows Server and keeps it synchronized in both directions with Azure Files, so the local server acts like a cache of your cloud file share. With cloud tiering, frequently accessed files stay on the local server and the rest stay in the cloud until someone requests them.`,keyFacts:[`AzCopy: command-line copying and one-direction sync for blobs and files.`,`Storage Explorer: a graphical app (Windows, macOS, Linux) that uses AzCopy underneath.`,`Azure File Sync: keeps a Windows file server and Azure Files in two-way sync, with cloud tiering.`],confusedWith:[{term:`AzCopy sync vs Azure File Sync`,difference:`AzCopy syncs one way, from source to destination; Azure File Sync keeps a Windows Server and Azure Files in sync both ways.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`AzCopy sync`,b:`Azure File Sync`,hint:`AzCopy syncs one way; File Sync keeps both sides in sync.`,aClue:`Copies one way, from a source to a destination`,bClue:`Keeps a Windows Server and a cloud share matched both ways`}}],learnUrl:`${l}/7-identify-azure-file-movement-options`}},{id:`6.6`,title:`Describe migration options, including Azure Migrate and Azure Data Box`,keyTerms:[`Azure Migrate`,`Azure Data Box`],notes:{explanation:`Azure offers two main ways to migrate into the cloud. Azure Migrate is a service that acts as a central hub for assessing and migrating an on-premises datacenter to Azure. It gives you one portal to start, run, and track the migration, with integrated tools for discovery and assessment, server migration, database migration, and web app migration, and it also works with tools from independent software vendors (ISVs). Azure Data Box is a physical migration service: Microsoft ships you a rugged storage device (the Learn unit gives up to 80 terabytes of usable capacity), you copy your data onto it, and a carrier returns it so the data can be uploaded to Azure. Data Box also works in reverse to export data from Azure. It suits moving very large data sets, often tens of terabytes or more, when network bandwidth is limited, such as a one-time bulk migration.`,keyFacts:[`Azure Migrate: a hub to discover, assess, and migrate servers, databases, and web apps to Azure.`,`Azure Data Box: a physical device shipped to you for offline bulk data transfer (Learn: up to 80 TB usable; see Verify).`,`Data Box suits very large data sets with limited bandwidth, and it supports both import and export.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure Migrate vs Azure Data Box`,difference:`Azure Migrate assesses and migrates workloads over the network; Data Box physically ships data when the network is too slow.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Azure Migrate`,b:`Azure Data Box`,hint:`Migrate works over the network; Data Box ships data physically.`,aClue:`Assesses and moves workloads over the network`,bClue:`A rugged device shipped to you when the network is too slow`}}],learnUrl:`${l}/6-identify-azure-data-migration-options`,verify:`The AZ-900 Learn unit gives Azure Data Box a maximum usable capacity of 80 TB. The current Azure Data Box documentation lists next-generation devices with 120 TB and 525 TB of usable capacity, and says the 80 TB device will be retired region by region as they roll out. Expect the exam to use the Learn figure, but don’t rely on 80 TB as the largest option.`}}]},d=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-azure-identity-access-security`,f={id:`identity-access-security`,number:7,domainId:`architecture-services`,title:`Identity, access, and security`,outlineHeading:`Describe Azure identity, access, and security`,objectives:[{id:`7.1`,title:`Describe directory services in Azure, including Microsoft Entra ID and Microsoft Entra Domain Services`,keyTerms:[`Microsoft Entra ID`,`Entra Domain Services`],notes:{explanation:`Microsoft Entra ID is Microsoft’s cloud-based identity and access management service. It signs users in to Microsoft cloud apps and to apps you build, and it provides authentication, single sign-on, application management, and device management. On-premises Active Directory Domain Services (AD DS) runs on domain controllers that you maintain yourself. Microsoft Entra Connect synchronizes user identities between on-premises Active Directory and Microsoft Entra ID, so users get one consistent identity, and connecting them lets Microsoft detect suspicious sign-ins. Microsoft Entra Domain Services is for legacy apps that can’t use modern authentication. It provides managed domain services, such as domain join, group policy, LDAP, and Kerberos/NTLM authentication, without you deploying or maintaining domain controllers; Azure runs two for you. Sync from Entra ID to Domain Services is one-way, so objects created in the managed domain don’t sync back.`,keyFacts:[`Microsoft Entra ID: cloud identity and access management (sign-in, SSO, app and device management).`,`Microsoft Entra Domain Services: managed domain join, group policy, LDAP, and Kerberos/NTLM without running domain controllers.`,`Microsoft Entra Connect syncs identities between on-premises Active Directory and Microsoft Entra ID.`,`Sync from Microsoft Entra ID to Entra Domain Services is one-way.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Entra ID vs Entra Domain Services vs AD DS`,difference:`Entra ID is cloud identity for modern apps; Entra Domain Services is a managed domain for legacy apps; AD DS is the on-premises directory you run yourself.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Entra ID`,b:`Entra Domain Services`,hint:`Entra ID: cloud identity. Domain Services: managed legacy domain.`,aClue:`Cloud sign-in for modern apps`,bClue:`Managed LDAP, Kerberos, and group policy for legacy apps`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/2-directory-services`}},{id:`7.2`,title:`Describe authentication methods in Azure, including single sign-on (SSO), multifactor authentication (MFA), and passwordless`,keyTerms:[`SSO`,`MFA`,`passwordless`],notes:{explanation:`Authentication establishes the identity of a person, service, or device by requiring credentials. It answers “who are you?”, while authorization decides what that identity is allowed to do. Single sign-on (SSO) lets a user sign in once and access multiple trusted applications. It reduces password sprawl and makes access easier to remove when roles change, but SSO is only as secure as the initial authenticator. Multifactor authentication (MFA) asks for an extra factor so a stolen password alone isn’t enough. Factors come from three categories: something you know (a password), something you have (a phone that receives a code), and something you are (a fingerprint or face scan). Passwordless authentication removes the password entirely, replacing it with a trusted device plus a biometric or PIN. Microsoft Entra ID supports three passwordless options: Windows Hello for Business, the Microsoft Authenticator app, and FIDO2 security keys.`,keyFacts:[`MFA factor categories: something you know, something you have, something you are.`,`SSO: sign in once to reach many apps; it’s only as secure as the initial authenticator.`,`Passwordless options: Windows Hello for Business, Microsoft Authenticator app, FIDO2 security keys.`,`Passwordless is high security and high convenience; passwords alone are low security.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Authentication vs authorization`,difference:`Authentication proves who you are; authorization decides what you’re allowed to do, for example through Azure RBAC.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Authentication`,b:`Authorization`,hint:`Authentication: who you are. Authorization: what you can do.`,aClue:`Proves who you are`,bClue:`Decides what you're allowed to do`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/3-authentication-methods`}},{id:`7.3`,title:`Describe external identities in Azure`,keyTerms:[`external identities`],notes:{explanation:`An external identity is a person, device, or service outside your organization’s tenant, such as a partner, supplier, contractor, or customer. Microsoft Entra External ID covers the capabilities for working with them securely. External users can bring their own identities, such as work accounts, government-issued digital identities, or social identities like Google or Facebook. Their identity provider handles authentication, and you control authorization to your apps. B2B collaboration lets external users sign in to your apps with their own identity; they usually appear in your directory as guest users. B2B direct connect sets up a two-way trust with another Microsoft Entra tenant, currently for Teams shared channels, and those users aren’t added to your directory. External ID for customers lets you publish apps to consumers. Access reviews let guests or a decision maker confirm whether each guest still needs access.`,keyFacts:[`External identities: people outside your tenant who use their own work, government, or social identities.`,`B2B collaboration: external users appear in your directory as guest users.`,`B2B direct connect: a two-way trust with another Microsoft Entra tenant (Teams shared channels); users aren’t added to your directory.`,`External ID for customers handles identity for consumer-facing apps; Microsoft Entra External ID is the next-generation successor to Azure AD B2C, not a rename of it.`],confusedWith:[{term:`B2B collaboration vs B2B direct connect`,difference:`B2B collaboration adds guest users to your directory; B2B direct connect uses a mutual trust and doesn’t add them.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`B2B collaboration`,b:`B2B direct connect`,hint:`Collaboration adds guest users; direct connect doesn't.`,aClue:`Adds external users to your directory as guests`,bClue:`Uses a mutual trust with another tenant and adds no guests`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/4-external-identities`,verify:`Microsoft Entra External ID is Microsoft’s next-generation external identity product, not a renamed Azure AD B2C. Azure AD B2C stopped being sold to new customers on May 1, 2025, but existing customers can keep using it, with support until at least May 2030. The Learn unit loosely calls External ID for customers “formerly Azure AD B2C”, so expect either name on the exam.`}},{id:`7.4`,title:`Describe Microsoft Entra Conditional Access`,keyTerms:[`Conditional Access`],notes:{explanation:`Microsoft Entra Conditional Access is a tool that Microsoft Entra ID uses to allow or deny access to resources based on identity signals, such as who the user is, where they are, and what device they’re using. During sign-in it works in three steps: it collects signals, makes a decision based on them, and enforces that decision by allowing access, requiring multifactor authentication, or blocking access. This makes MFA more granular. A user at a known location might not be challenged, while a sign-in from an unusual or high-risk location might need a second factor or be blocked entirely. Common uses include requiring MFA for administrators or for connections from outside trusted networks, allowing only approved client applications, requiring managed devices that meet your security standards, and blocking access from unknown or unexpected locations.`,keyFacts:[`Conditional Access flow: signals → decision → enforcement.`,`Signals include the user or their role, location, device, and the application being accessed.`,`Possible outcomes: allow access, require MFA, or block access.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Conditional Access vs RBAC`,difference:`Conditional Access decides whether a sign-in is allowed, based on signals; RBAC decides what an identity can do to Azure resources.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Conditional Access`,b:`Azure RBAC`,hint:`CA: is this sign-in allowed? RBAC: what can this identity do?`,aClue:`Decides whether a sign-in is allowed, based on signals`,bClue:`Decides what an identity can do to resources`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/5-conditional-access`}},{id:`7.5`,title:`Describe Azure role-based access control (RBAC)`,keyTerms:[`RBAC`],notes:{explanation:`Azure role-based access control (Azure RBAC) applies the principle of least privilege without managing permissions person by person. Azure provides built-in roles, such as Owner and Reader, and you can define your own. Assign users or groups to a role and they get all of its permissions, so a new engineer added to the engineers group gets the same access as everyone else in it. A role is assigned at a scope: a management group, a subscription, a resource group, or a single resource. RBAC is hierarchical, so permissions granted at a parent scope are inherited by every child scope; Reader on a subscription, for example, lets you view every resource in it. RBAC is enforced on actions that go through Azure Resource Manager, not at the application or data level. It uses an allow model, so permissions from multiple role assignments add together.`,keyFacts:[`Scopes: management group, subscription, resource group, or a single resource.`,`Permissions granted at a parent scope are inherited by all child scopes.`,`Allow model: permissions from multiple role assignments add up (read + write = both).`,`Enforced through Azure Resource Manager, not at the application or data level.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Conditional Access`,difference:`RBAC grants permissions on Azure resources; Conditional Access decides whether a sign-in is allowed in the first place.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Azure RBAC`,b:`Conditional Access`,hint:`RBAC grants resource permissions; CA gates the sign-in itself.`,exclude:`Same pair as 7.4, reversed`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/6-role-based-access-control`}},{id:`7.6`,title:`Describe the concept of Zero Trust`,keyTerms:[`Zero Trust`],notes:{explanation:`Zero Trust is a security model that assumes the worst-case scenario: it assumes a breach has already happened and verifies each request as though it came from an uncontrolled network. Microsoft recommends it, and it rests on three guiding principles. Verify explicitly: always authenticate and authorize based on all available data points. Use least privilege access: limit user access with just-in-time and just-enough-access (JIT/JEA), risk-based adaptive policies, and data protection. Assume breach: limit the potential impact and segment access, verify end-to-end encryption, and use analytics to detect threats. Traditional networks assumed that anything inside the corporate perimeter was safe. Zero Trust flips that: everyone must authenticate, and access is granted based on authentication rather than location. A sign-in from an unmanaged device on a public network is still checked for identity, device state, and risk before access is allowed.`,keyFacts:[`Three principles: verify explicitly, use least privilege access, assume breach.`,`Access is granted based on authentication and context, not on network location.`,`Least privilege uses just-in-time and just-enough-access (JIT/JEA).`],confusedWith:[{term:`Perimeter-based security`,difference:`Perimeter security trusts anything inside the corporate network; Zero Trust verifies every request wherever it comes from.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Zero Trust`,b:`Perimeter security`,hint:`Perimeter trusts the inside; Zero Trust verifies every request.`,aClue:`Verifies every request, wherever it comes from`,bClue:`Treats anything inside the corporate network as safe`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/7-describe-zero-trust-model`}},{id:`7.7`,title:`Describe the purpose of the defense-in-depth model`,keyTerms:[`defense in depth`],notes:{explanation:`Defense in depth protects information with a series of security layers, with the data being protected at the center. If one layer is breached, the next is already in place, so no single layer is relied on, attacks slow down, and security teams get alerts they can act on. Microsoft Learn lists seven layers, from the outside in. Physical security protects datacenter buildings and hardware. Identity and access controls access to infrastructure, using SSO and MFA, and audits changes. Perimeter uses DDoS protection and perimeter firewalls against large-scale network attacks. Network limits communication between resources and denies by default. Compute secures access to virtual machines and keeps systems patched. Application keeps apps free of vulnerabilities and stores secrets in Azure Key Vault. Data controls access to the data itself, which is protected with encryption at rest and in transit.`,keyFacts:[`Layers, outside in: physical security → identity and access → perimeter → network → compute → application → data.`,`Data sits at the center, because attackers are almost always after data.`,`DDoS protection belongs to the perimeter layer; “deny by default” belongs to the network layer.`,`If one layer is breached, the next layer is still protecting the data.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Defense in depth vs Zero Trust`,difference:`Defense in depth stacks protective layers around data; Zero Trust verifies every access request instead of trusting network location.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Defense in depth`,b:`Zero Trust`,hint:`Defense in depth: layers around data. Zero Trust: verify always.`,aClue:`Stacks protective layers around the data`,bClue:`Checks every access request instead of relying on location`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/8-describe-defense-depth`}},{id:`7.8`,title:`Describe the purpose of Microsoft Defender for Cloud`,keyTerms:[`Microsoft Defender for Cloud`],notes:{explanation:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud is a security posture management and threat protection service. It monitors cloud, on-premises, hybrid, and multicloud resources and gives recommendations and alerts to improve your security posture. Because it’s native to Azure, many Azure services can be monitored without extra deployment. To cover on-premises machines, you deploy Azure Arc and enable Defender for Cloud’s enhanced security features, and it can also protect resources in other clouds, including AWS and Google Cloud (GCP). Defender for Cloud meets three needs. Continuously assess: know your security posture and track vulnerabilities. Secure: harden resources against the Microsoft cloud security benchmark, with recommendations grouped into security controls and summarized as a secure score. Defend: detect threats and raise security alerts that describe the affected resources and suggest remediation steps.`,keyFacts:[`Security posture management plus threat protection for Azure, on-premises, hybrid, and multicloud (AWS, GCP).`,`Three jobs: continuously assess, secure, and defend.`,`The secure score summarizes posture; recommendations align to the Microsoft cloud security benchmark.`,`On-premises machines are covered by deploying Azure Arc.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure Policy`,difference:`Defender for Cloud builds on Azure Policy: Policy enforces rules on resources, while Defender for Cloud assesses overall security posture and raises threat alerts.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Defender for Cloud`,b:`Azure Policy`,hint:`Policy enforces rules; Defender assesses posture, raises alerts.`,aClue:`Assesses security posture and raises threat alerts`,bClue:`Enforces rules on resources, such as allowed locations`}}],learnUrl:`${d}/9-describe-microsoft-defender-for-cloud`}}]},p=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-cost-management-azure`,m={id:`cost-management`,number:8,domainId:`management-governance`,title:`Cost management`,outlineHeading:`Describe cost management in Azure`,objectives:[{id:`8.1`,title:`Describe factors that can affect costs in Azure`,keyTerms:[`factors that affect cost`],notes:{explanation:`Azure charges for what you use, so many factors affect the bill. Resource type and settings matter: a storage account’s price depends on the kind of data, access speed, number of backup copies, and region, and a VM’s depends on licensing, processor cores, attached storage, and network interface. Consumption matters: pay-as-you-go charges for what you use each billing cycle, while reservations, savings plans for compute, and Spot VMs lower costs for suitable workloads. Maintenance matters, because deleting a VM may leave its storage and networking resources behind, still billing. Geography matters, since prices differ by region because of power, labor, taxes, and fees. Network traffic matters too: some inbound data transfer is free, while outbound transfer is priced by billing zone. Subscription type, such as a free trial with credits, and Azure Marketplace purchases also affect cost.`,keyFacts:[`Cost factors: resource type, consumption, maintenance, geography, subscription type, and Azure Marketplace.`,`Some inbound data transfer (into Azure datacenters) is free; outbound transfer (leaving Azure datacenters) is priced by billing zone.`,`The same resource can cost different amounts in different regions.`,`Deprovisioning a VM may leave its storage and networking resources behind, still costing money.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Billing zones vs availability zones`,difference:`A billing zone is a group of regions used for data-transfer pricing; an availability zone is a physically separate part of one region.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Billing zone`,b:`Availability zone`,hint:`A billing zone groups regions for data-transfer pricing.`,aClue:`A group of regions used to price data transfer`,bClue:`A physically separate part of one region`}}],learnUrl:`${p}/2-describe-factors-affect-costs-azure`}},{id:`8.2`,title:`Explore the pricing calculator`,keyTerms:[`pricing calculator`],notes:{explanation:`The pricing calculator is an online tool that estimates the cost of provisioning resources in Azure. You can price individual resources, build out a whole solution, or start from an example scenario, and you can include compute, storage, and associated network costs. You can also compare options such as storage type, access tier, and redundancy, or see how an estimate changes across regions and service tiers. The calculator is for information purposes only: prices are estimates, nothing is provisioned when you add resources, and you aren’t charged for anything you select. Teams often use the estimate as a baseline for budget approval before deploying, then revisit it when the design changes. On the exam, the pricing calculator is the answer for estimating costs before you deploy. Tracking and controlling what you actually spend afterward is the job of Microsoft Cost Management.`,keyFacts:[`The pricing calculator estimates the cost of Azure resources before you deploy them.`,`Nothing is provisioned and nothing is charged; prices are estimates only.`,`You can compare regions, service tiers, storage types, access tiers, and redundancy options.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Microsoft Cost Management`,difference:`The pricing calculator estimates costs before you deploy; Cost Management analyzes and controls actual spending after you deploy.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Pricing calculator`,b:`Cost Management`,hint:`The calculator estimates first; Cost Management tracks actuals.`,aClue:`Estimates spending before you deploy`,bClue:`Analyzes and controls actual spending after you deploy`}},{term:`TCO calculator`,difference:`Microsoft Learn says the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) calculator has been retired; the outline names only the pricing calculator.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Pricing calculator`,b:`TCO calculator`,hint:`Microsoft Learn says the TCO calculator has been retired.`,exclude:`The TCO calculator is retired, so there's no current fact to write a clue from.`}}],learnUrl:`${p}/3-compare-pricing-total-cost-of-ownership-calculators`,verify:`Microsoft Learn says the Total Cost of Ownership (TCO) calculator has been retired. Older study guides and practice questions may still mention it; the current outline names only the pricing calculator.`}},{id:`8.3`,title:`Describe cost management capabilities in Azure`,keyTerms:[`cost management tools`],notes:{explanation:`Microsoft Cost Management is a set of tools for analyzing, monitoring, and managing Azure costs, so surprises such as accidentally provisioned resources don’t wait until the invoice. Cost analysis gives a quick visual of your costs by billing cycle, region, resource, subscription, resource group, or service, and shows accumulated costs over time against a budget. A budget sets a spending limit for a subscription, resource group, service type, or other criteria, and comes with a budget alert threshold. Budgets can also trigger automation, such as shutting down nonproduction resources. Cost alerts collect three alert types in one place. Budget alerts fire when spending reaches a threshold you set. Credit alerts fire as an Enterprise Agreement’s Azure Prepayment is used up, at 90% and 100%. Department spending quota alerts tell Enterprise Agreement department owners when spending reaches a set share of their quota.`,keyFacts:[`Cost analysis visualizes actual costs by region, resource, subscription, resource group, service, and more.`,`Budgets set a spending limit and trigger budget alerts; they can also trigger automation.`,`Three cost alert types: budget alerts, credit alerts, and department spending quota alerts.`,`Credit alerts and department spending quota alerts apply to Enterprise Agreement customers.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Budget vs spending cap`,difference:`A budget alerts you at a threshold and can trigger automation, but on its own it doesn’t stop resources or block spending.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Budget`,b:`Spending cap`,hint:`A budget alerts and can automate; it doesn't block spending.`,aClue:`Alerts at a threshold and can trigger automation`,bClue:`Blocks further charges once the limit is reached`}}],learnUrl:`${p}/6-describe-azure-tool`}},{id:`8.4`,title:`Describe the purpose of tags`,keyTerms:[`tags`],notes:{explanation:`Tags are name-value pairs of metadata that you attach to Azure resources, such as Environment = Prod or CostCenter = 1234. They’re a way to organize resources alongside subscriptions and resource groups. Learn lists their uses: resource management, cost management and optimization, operations management, security classification, governance and regulatory compliance, and workload optimization and automation. For cost, tags let you group resources to report costs, allocate internal cost centers, track budgets, and forecast spending. You can add, change, or delete tags in the portal, PowerShell, the Azure CLI, ARM templates, or the REST API. Azure Policy can enforce tagging, for example by requiring a tag on new resources or reapplying a tag that was removed. Resources don’t inherit tags from their subscription or resource group, so tags applied at one level don’t automatically appear at another.`,keyFacts:[`Tags are name-value metadata pairs that you can apply at the resource, resource group, or subscription level.`,`Main uses include cost reporting, resource management, operations, security classification, compliance, and automation.`,`Resources don’t inherit tags from their resource group or subscription.`,`Azure Policy can require tags on new resources or reapply tags that were removed.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Tags vs resource groups`,difference:`A resource belongs to only one resource group but can carry many tags, so tags can group resources across resource groups and subscriptions.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Tags`,b:`Resource groups`,hint:`One resource group per resource, but it can carry many tags.`,aClue:`Name-value labels; one item can carry many of them`,bClue:`Each VM or database belongs to exactly one`}}],learnUrl:`${p}/7-describe-purpose-of-tags`}}]},h=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-features-tools-azure-for-governance-compliance`,g={id:`governance-compliance`,number:9,domainId:`management-governance`,title:`Governance and compliance`,outlineHeading:`Describe features and tools in Azure for governance and compliance`,objectives:[{id:`9.1`,title:`Describe the purpose of Microsoft Purview in Azure`,keyTerms:[`Microsoft Purview`],notes:{explanation:`Microsoft Purview is a family of data governance, risk, and compliance solutions that gives you a single, unified view of your data, whether it lives on-premises, in multiple clouds, or in software-as-a-service apps. It keeps you current on your data landscape through automated data discovery, sensitive data classification, and end-to-end data lineage. Purview has two main solution areas. Risk and compliance solutions build on Microsoft 365 services such as Teams, OneDrive, and Exchange to protect sensitive data across clouds, apps, and devices, identify data risks, and manage regulatory compliance requirements. Unified data governance helps you map your entire data estate, including data in Azure, SQL and Hive databases, on-premises, and other clouds such as Amazon S3. It also helps you find where sensitive data is stored, help data consumers find valuable data securely, and manage access to data at scale.`,keyFacts:[`Microsoft Purview: data governance, risk, and compliance for on-premises, multicloud, and SaaS data.`,`Two main solution areas: risk and compliance, and unified data governance.`,`Capabilities include automated data discovery, sensitive data classification, and end-to-end data lineage.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure Policy`,difference:`Azure Policy governs how Azure resources are configured; Microsoft Purview governs the data itself, wherever it is stored.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Microsoft Purview`,b:`Azure Policy`,hint:`Purview governs data; Azure Policy governs resource settings.`,aClue:`Governs the data itself, wherever it's stored`,bClue:`Governs how resources are configured`}}],learnUrl:`${h}/2-describe-purpose-microsoft-purview`}},{id:`9.2`,title:`Describe the purpose of Azure Policy`,keyTerms:[`Azure Policy`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Policy is a service for creating, assigning, and managing policies that control or audit your resources, so their configurations stay compliant with your standards. It evaluates resources and highlights the ones that aren’t compliant, and it can prevent noncompliant resources from being created. Policies can be set at any level, such as a subscription, a resource group, or a single resource, and they’re inherited by everything below. An initiative groups related policy definitions to track compliance with a larger goal. Policy also evaluates resources that existed before the policy was created. For example, a policy that allows only certain VM sizes checks new VMs, resized VMs, and all current VMs. Existing noncompliant resources are flagged. In some cases Azure Policy can fix them automatically, such as adding a missing tag, and resources can be marked as exceptions.`,keyFacts:[`Azure Policy enforces or audits rules on resource configurations and can block noncompliant resources from being created.`,`An initiative is a group of related policy definitions for a larger compliance goal.`,`Policies are inherited by child scopes and also evaluate resources that already existed.`,`Existing noncompliant resources are flagged; some can be remediated automatically, such as adding a missing tag.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure RBAC`,difference:`RBAC controls what users can do; Azure Policy controls how resources may be configured, no matter who creates them.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Azure Policy`,b:`Azure RBAC`,hint:`Policy: what resources may be. RBAC: what users may do.`,aClue:`Controls what resources may look like, whoever creates them`,bClue:`Controls what users are allowed to do`}}],learnUrl:`${h}/3-describe-purpose-azure-policy`}},{id:`9.3`,title:`Describe the purpose of resource locks`,keyTerms:[`resource locks`],notes:{explanation:`A resource lock prevents Azure resources from being accidentally deleted or changed. Even with Azure RBAC in place, people with enough access could still delete critical resources, so locks add protection on top. There are two lock types. A Delete lock lets authorized users read and modify a resource but not delete it. A ReadOnly lock lets authorized users read a resource but not update or delete it, which is similar to limiting everyone to the Reader role. Locks can be applied to a single resource, a resource group, or a whole subscription, and they’re inherited, so a lock on a resource group applies to every resource in it. Locks apply regardless of RBAC permissions: even an Owner must remove the lock before performing the blocked action. Changing or deleting a locked resource is a two-step process: remove the lock, then act.`,keyFacts:[`Two lock types: Delete (you can modify but not delete) and ReadOnly (you can read but not modify or delete).`,`Locks apply regardless of RBAC permissions, including to Owners.`,`To delete or change a locked resource, you must first remove the lock.`,`Locks can be set on a resource, resource group, or subscription, and they are inherited.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Resource locks vs RBAC`,difference:`RBAC decides who may act on a resource; a lock blocks delete or change actions for everyone, even users whose role allows them.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Resource lock`,b:`Azure RBAC`,hint:`A lock blocks delete or change for everyone, even Owners.`,aClue:`Blocks delete or change for everyone, even Owners`,bClue:`Decides who may act, through role assignments`}}],learnUrl:`${h}/4-describe-purpose-resource-locks`}}]},_=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-features-tools-manage-deploy-azure-resources`,v={id:`managing-deploying`,number:10,domainId:`management-governance`,title:`Managing and deploying resources`,outlineHeading:`Describe features and tools for managing and deploying Azure resources`,objectives:[{id:`10.1`,title:`Describe the Azure portal`,keyTerms:[`Azure portal`],notes:{explanation:`The Azure portal is a web-based, unified console for managing your Azure subscription through a graphical user interface, as an alternative to command-line tools. In the portal you can build, manage, and monitor everything from simple web apps to complex cloud deployments, create custom dashboards for an organized view of your resources, and configure accessibility options. The portal is designed for resiliency and continuous availability. It has a presence in every Azure datacenter, which makes it resilient to individual datacenter failures and keeps it close to users, and it updates continuously with no downtime for maintenance. It’s also where you open Azure Cloud Shell, by selecting the Cloud Shell icon. On the exam, choose the portal when a scenario calls for a graphical, point-and-click interface; choose the Azure CLI or Azure PowerShell when it calls for scripting or automation.`,keyFacts:[`The Azure portal is a web-based graphical console for managing Azure.`,`You can build, manage, and monitor resources and create custom dashboards.`,`It has a presence in every Azure datacenter and needs no downtime for maintenance.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Portal vs command-line tools`,difference:`The portal is a point-and-click interface; the Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell let you capture tasks in repeatable, automatable scripts.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Azure portal`,b:`Azure CLI`,hint:`Portal: point-and-click. CLI: repeatable, scriptable commands.`,aClue:`A point-and-click web console`,bClue:`Bash commands you can script and repeat`}}],learnUrl:`${_}/2-describe-interacting-azure`}},{id:`10.2`,title:`Describe Azure Cloud Shell, Azure CLI, and Azure PowerShell`,keyTerms:[`Cloud Shell`,`Azure CLI`,`Azure PowerShell`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Cloud Shell is a browser-based shell for creating, configuring, and managing Azure resources. It needs no local installation, and it’s already authenticated with your Azure credentials, so it knows who you are and what permissions you have. You open it from the Azure portal and choose the shell you prefer: Azure PowerShell or the Azure CLI. Azure PowerShell runs commands called cmdlets, which call the Azure REST API. The Azure CLI does the same jobs using Bash commands. The two are functionally equivalent; the main difference is command syntax, so the choice mostly comes down to which language you know. Both can handle one-off changes or orchestrate complex deployments from scripts, which makes the work repeatable and automatable. Both can also be installed on Windows, Linux, and macOS as well as used in Cloud Shell.`,keyFacts:[`Cloud Shell: browser-based, no install, already authenticated; offers Azure PowerShell or the Azure CLI (Bash).`,`Azure PowerShell runs cmdlets; the Azure CLI runs Bash commands.`,`Azure PowerShell and the Azure CLI are functionally equivalent; the difference is syntax.`,`Both install on Windows, Linux, and macOS, and both support scripting for automation.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Cloud Shell vs Azure CLI`,difference:`Cloud Shell is where you run commands, in the browser; the Azure CLI and Azure PowerShell are the command sets you run in it.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Cloud Shell`,b:`Azure CLI`,hint:`Cloud Shell is where you run CLI or PowerShell, in a browser.`,aClue:`A browser-based place to run commands, already signed in`,bClue:`A command set that uses Bash syntax`}}],learnUrl:`${_}/2-describe-interacting-azure`}},{id:`10.3`,title:`Describe the purpose of Azure Arc`,keyTerms:[`Azure Arc`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Arc extends Azure management, governance, and monitoring to resources outside Azure: in your on-premises datacenter and in other public clouds. It works with Azure Resource Manager by projecting your existing non-Azure resources into it, so you can manage them as if they were running in Azure, with the same Azure services and tools. That gives you one consistent way to apply governance, policy, and inventory tracking across Azure, on-premises, and multicloud environments, instead of using disconnected tools for each. Learn lists the resource types Azure Arc can currently manage outside Azure: servers, Kubernetes clusters, Azure data services, SQL Server, and virtual machines (in preview). Azure Arc shows up elsewhere in AZ-900 too. It’s how Microsoft Defender for Cloud protects on-premises machines, and it’s the tool Learn names for managing hybrid and multicloud environments.`,keyFacts:[`Azure Arc extends Azure management and governance to on-premises and other clouds.`,`It projects non-Azure resources into Azure Resource Manager so you can manage them like Azure resources.`,`It manages servers, Kubernetes clusters, Azure data services, SQL Server, and VMs (preview) outside Azure.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure Arc vs Azure Migrate`,difference:`Azure Arc manages resources where they already run; Azure Migrate moves workloads into Azure.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Azure Arc`,b:`Azure Migrate`,hint:`Arc manages resources where they run; Migrate moves them.`,aClue:`Manages resources where they already run`,bClue:`Moves on-premises workloads into Microsoft's cloud`}}],learnUrl:`${_}/3-describe-purpose-of-azure-arc`,verify:`Learn lists virtual machines as a preview capability of Azure Arc. Preview features can change or become generally available, so check the current Azure Arc docs before relying on it.`}},{id:`10.4`,title:`Describe infrastructure as code (IaC)`,keyTerms:[`infrastructure as code`],notes:{explanation:`Infrastructure as code (IaC) means managing infrastructure through code and templates instead of manual configuration. Because the definition lives in code, you can deploy the same environment again and again with consistent results. Learn describes a progression: IaC can start with scripts in the Azure CLI or Azure PowerShell and grow into repeatable environment deployments with Azure Resource Manager templates and Bicep. The difference to know is imperative versus declarative. Scripts are imperative: they list the step-by-step commands to run. ARM templates and Bicep are declarative: you describe the end state you want, meaning what to deploy rather than how, and Azure Resource Manager works out the order, deploys resources in parallel where possible, and handles dependencies. Deploying the same template again gives consistent results, and Learn describes Bicep files as idempotent.`,keyFacts:[`IaC = managing infrastructure through code and templates instead of manual configuration.`,`Imperative (Azure CLI or PowerShell scripts): step-by-step commands. Declarative (ARM templates, Bicep): describe the end state.`,`Declarative templates give repeatable, consistent deployments; Bicep files are idempotent.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Imperative vs declarative`,difference:`Imperative code says how to deploy, step by step; declarative code says what should exist and lets Azure Resource Manager work out how.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Imperative`,b:`Declarative`,hint:`Imperative: how, step by step. Declarative: what should exist.`,aClue:`Says how, step by step, like a script of commands`,bClue:`Describes the end state and lets the platform work out how`}}],learnUrl:`${_}/4-describe-azure-resource-manager-azure-arm-templates`}},{id:`10.5`,title:`Describe Azure Resource Manager (ARM) and ARM templates`,keyTerms:[`ARM and ARM templates`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Resource Manager (ARM) is the deployment and management service for Azure. Every request from any Azure tool, API, or SDK goes through it: ARM authenticates and authorizes the request, then sends it to the Azure service that carries it out. Because everything uses the same API, you get consistent results from the portal, CLI, PowerShell, or templates. ARM lets you deploy, manage, and monitor a solution’s resources as a group, define dependencies so resources deploy in the right order, apply RBAC natively, and apply tags. ARM templates are JSON files that describe the resources you want declaratively. Azure validates a template before deploying it, then orchestrates creation in the right order and in parallel where possible. Templates are repeatable across environments and modular, and they can call PowerShell or Bash deployment scripts. Bicep is a simpler declarative language that also deploys through ARM.`,keyFacts:[`Azure Resource Manager is the management layer every Azure request goes through; it authenticates and authorizes requests.`,`ARM templates are declarative JSON files that define what to deploy.`,`ARM handles dependency order and parallel deployment automatically.`,`Bicep is a simpler, more concise declarative language that deploys through ARM.`],confusedWith:[{term:`ARM vs ARM templates vs Bicep`,difference:`ARM is the service that deploys and manages resources; ARM templates (JSON) and Bicep are two languages for telling it what to deploy.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Resource Manager`,b:`ARM template`,hint:`ARM is the service; templates and Bicep describe what to deploy.`,aClue:`The service every Azure request goes through`,bClue:`A JSON file describing what to deploy`}}],learnUrl:`${_}/4-describe-azure-resource-manager-azure-arm-templates`}}]},y=`https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/training/modules/describe-monitoring-tools-azure`,b=[e,t,n,a,c,u,f,m,g,v,{id:`monitoring`,number:11,domainId:`management-governance`,title:`Monitoring`,outlineHeading:`Describe monitoring tools in Azure`,objectives:[{id:`11.1`,title:`Describe the purpose of Azure Advisor`,keyTerms:[`Azure Advisor`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Advisor evaluates your Azure resources and recommends ways to improve them. Think of it as a personalized best-practices guide built into the Azure portal. The Advisor dashboard shows recommendations for all your subscriptions, and you can filter by subscription, resource group, or service. Each recommendation includes a suggested action you can take right away, postpone, or dismiss, and you can set up notifications for new recommendations. Recommendations fall into five categories. Reliability flags configuration risks that could stop your applications running. Security detects threats and vulnerabilities that could lead to breaches. Performance identifies changes that can speed up your applications. Operational excellence suggests workflow and deployment improvements. Cost finds ways to reduce your Azure spending. Advisor also includes the recommendations from Microsoft Defender for Cloud, so the two services show overlapping security advice.`,keyFacts:[`Azure Advisor gives personalized best-practice recommendations for your Azure resources.`,`Five categories: reliability, security, performance, operational excellence, and cost.`,`You can act on, postpone, or dismiss each recommendation.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Microsoft Defender for Cloud`,difference:`Advisor covers five areas and includes Defender for Cloud’s recommendations; Defender for Cloud focuses only on security posture and threat protection.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Azure Advisor`,b:`Defender for Cloud`,hint:`Advisor: 5 areas, including Defender's security advice.`,aClue:`Recommends cost, performance, and reliability improvements`,bClue:`Gives a secure score and raises threat alerts`}}],learnUrl:`${y}/2-describe-purpose-of-azure-advisor`}},{id:`11.2`,title:`Describe Azure Service Health`,keyTerms:[`Service Health`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Service Health keeps you informed about the health of Azure itself and of the resources you run. It combines three views that narrow from global to individual. Azure status is the global picture across all Azure services and regions, useful when you hear about a widespread outage. Service Health focuses on the services and regions you actually use. Because you’re signed in, it shows the outages, planned maintenance, and health advisories that affect your environment, and you can set alerts so you’re notified automatically. Resource Health zooms in on a single resource, such as one virtual machine, and tells you whether it’s running normally and whether a problem is on Azure’s side or yours. Historical alerts are kept so you can spot recurring trends, and when an event affects your workloads, Service Health links you to support.`,keyFacts:[`Azure status: the global health of all Azure services and regions.`,`Service Health: outages, planned maintenance, and advisories for the services and regions you use, with alerts.`,`Resource Health: the health of one resource, and whether an issue is on Azure’s side or yours.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure status vs Service Health vs Resource Health`,difference:`Azure status covers all of Azure; Service Health covers the services and regions you use; Resource Health covers one specific resource.`,source:`learn`,match:{a:`Service Health`,b:`Resource Health`,hint:`Service Health: services you use. Resource Health: one resource.`,aClue:`Outages and maintenance for the regions you use`,bClue:`Whether one VM is running normally`}}],learnUrl:`${y}/3-describe-azure-service-health`}},{id:`11.3`,title:`Describe Azure Monitor, including Log Analytics, Azure Monitor alerts, and Azure Monitor Application Insights`,keyTerms:[`Azure Monitor`,`Log Analytics`,`alerts`,`Application Insights`],notes:{explanation:`Azure Monitor is a platform for collecting, analyzing, and acting on data from your resources and applications, in Azure, on-premises, or in other clouds. It gathers logs and metrics from applications, operating systems, and network layers, stores them centrally, and makes them available through dashboards, queries, and alerts. Log Analytics is the tool in the Azure portal where you write and run queries against that data, from simple filters, like all errors in the last hour, to advanced trend analysis. Azure Monitor alerts notify you when a condition you define is met. An alert rule sets the condition, and an action group controls who gets notified and what happens next. Alerts can be metric-based or log-based. Application Insights is the Azure Monitor feature for web applications: it tracks request rates, response times, failures, dependencies, page loads, and users, and it can run availability tests.`,keyFacts:[`Azure Monitor collects and analyzes logs and metrics from Azure, on-premises, and multicloud resources.`,`Log Analytics: write and run queries against the collected log data.`,`Alerts: an alert rule defines the condition and an action group defines who is notified; alerts can be metric-based or log-based.`,`Application Insights: monitors web app performance and usage, including availability tests.`],confusedWith:[{term:`Azure Monitor vs Service Health`,difference:`Azure Monitor tracks the performance and logs of your own resources and apps; Service Health reports on the health of the Azure services you use.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Azure Monitor`,b:`Service Health`,hint:`Monitor: your resources' data. Service Health: Azure's health.`,aClue:`Collects logs and metrics from your own resources`,bClue:`Reports outages and planned maintenance affecting you`}},{term:`Log Analytics vs Application Insights`,difference:`Log Analytics is where you query collected log data; Application Insights collects performance and usage data from web applications.`,source:`framing`,match:{a:`Log Analytics`,b:`Application Insights`,hint:`Log Analytics queries logs; App Insights watches web apps.`,aClue:`Where you write queries against collected data`,bClue:`Tracks a web app's response times, failures, and users`}}],learnUrl:`${y}/4-describe-azure-monitor`}}]}],x=new Map(b.map(e=>[e.id,e]));function S(e){return x.get(e)}function C(e){return`placeholder`in e}export{b as n,S as r,C as t};